    description: "LIVE BEHAVIORAL REPRO, 2026-08-20, dev @4ff442c5b0. Found while pricing\nINV-kahig's option A (does ruby's constructor edge need to stay in the calls\nfamily?) -- the pricing question turned out to be a live defect independent of\nruby.\n\nTHE A/B. Two fixtures, identical except for the SPELLING of the sink:\n\n    # both arms\n    import subprocess, sys\n    def handle_request() -> None:\n        data = sys.stdin.read()      # ipc_recv -> untrusted_input\n        subprocess.run(data)         # CONTROL\n        subprocess.Popen(data)       # SUBJECT\n\nRun against the shipped generic claims file, claim\n`untrusted-input-no-subprocess` (\"this is the command-injection question\"):\n\n    hypergumbo verify-claims <arm> --claims docs/example-claims/generic-taint-claims.yaml --format json\n\n    CONTROL (run)    verdict=violated                 exit 1  evidence_count=2\n                     analysis_methods={\"ddg\": 2}, confidence=precise\n                     stdin -> run, read -> run\n\n    SUBJECT (Popen)  verdict=confirmed_with_caveats   exit 3  evidence_count=0\n                     analysis_methods={}\n                     details: \"No unsanitized untrusted_input data reaches\n                               subprocess zone.\"\n\nThe subject arm affirmatively states that no untrusted data reaches the\nsubprocess zone, about a two-line function in which untrusted data reaches the\nsubprocess zone.\n\nROOT CAUSE, read end to end.\n\n1. `python.yaml` catalogues subprocess sinks as\n   `functions: [run, Popen, call, check_call, check_output]` -- Popen IS a\n   catalogued sink, under a call-shape key.\n2. `py.py:5942` types `module.Attr()` where the attribute is PascalCase as\n   `edge_type=\"instantiates\"` (+ `evidence_type=\"ast_new\"`, `meta=None`), and\n   as `edge_type=\"calls\"` otherwise. This is the WI-jubag heuristic. `Popen` is\n   PascalCase; `run` is not. That single character is the whole difference.\n3. `TAINT_CALL_EDGE_TYPES` (taint.py:1604) is\n   `frozenset({\"calls\", \"module_attr_ref\", \"dispatches_to\"})` -- it does NOT\n   contain `instantiates`, and `taint.py` does not contain the string\n   `instantiates` ANYWHERE. `_is_taint_call_edge` is a plain membership test.\n4. So the edge carrying the tainted argument into Popen is not a taint-call\n   edge, the propagation walk never traverses it, and the sink is never\n   reached.\n\nWHY THE CAVEAT MAKES IT WORSE, NOT BETTER. The subject arm is not silent -- it\nemits an `opaque_boundary` caveat naming `subprocess.Popen` and saying\n\"hypergumbo cannot see inside a launched program, so what those programs do is\nnot covered by this verdict.\" That is TRUE and IRRELEVANT: the risk here is not\nwhat the launched program does, it is that attacker-controlled data chose the\ncommand line. The caveat makes the verdict read as carefully qualified while\nthe qualification names the wrong hazard, so a reader who checks the caveats is\nactively reassured.\n\nSCOPE -- NOT MEASURED, do not assume Popen-only. The defect is structural in\nstep 2/3, so it applies to ANY catalogued sink whose callee name is PascalCase\nand which is reached as `module.Name(...)`. Popen is the instance measured.\nEnumerating the rest of the catalogue for PascalCase `functions:` entries\nacross every language is the first task under this item. Note also that\n`io_boundary._TRACEABLE_EDGE_TYPES` and `verify_claims._CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES`\nBOTH already include `instantiates` -- so the boundary is tagged and the call\nsite is counted; it is specifically the taint PROPAGATION set that diverges.\nThree sets naming the same concept, two agree and one does not.\n\nRELATION TO THE REGISTRY. WI-toruz (merged 4ff442c5b0) declared\n`call_construct.applicable_edge_types = frozenset({\"calls\", \"instantiates\"})` --\ni.e. the registry now states that these two edge types ARE the call family.\n`TAINT_CALL_EDGE_TYPES` is a hand-maintained literal that disagrees with that\ndeclaration. That is the INV-nosoz shape (a consumer holding its own copy of a\nset the registry owns) one layer down, and it is the reason the fix should\nderive the set rather than add one more string to it.\n\nNOT A DUPLICATE of INV-linub (that class is about a call site not REACHING its\ncatalogue entry because of receiver syntax; here the catalogue entry is found\nfine -- io_boundary tags the Popen boundary -- and it is the taint walk that\ncannot traverse the edge) or of INV-nizom (absent call_construct, not a missing\nedge type)."  # 3694
    fields:  # 3694
      statement: "A catalogued sink reached with tainted data produces a taint finding regardless of whether the analyzer typed the call edge as calls or instantiates, so the edge_type chosen for a construction-shaped call never decides whether a command-injection flow is visible."  # 3694
      root_cause: "py.py:5942 types module.Attr() as edge_type=instantiates when the attribute is PascalCase and calls otherwise (the WI-jubag heuristic), while TAINT_CALL_EDGE_TYPES at taint.py:1604 is frozenset({calls, module_attr_ref, dispatches_to}) and taint.py never mentions instantiates at all. subprocess.Popen is a catalogued subprocess sink whose name is PascalCase, so the edge carrying tainted data into it is not a taint-call edge and the propagation walk never traverses it. io_boundary._TRACEABLE_EDGE_TYPES and verify_claims._CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES both DO include instantiates, so three sets naming the call family disagree and only the taint one is wrong."  # 3694
- op: discuss  # 53dc
  at: '2026-08-20T22:16:34Z'  # 53dc
  by: agent  # 53dc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 53dc
  clock: 2  # 53dc
  nonce: 53dc  # 53dc
  message: "SCOPE ENUMERATED (2026-08-20), the first task this item names. Swept every io_primitives catalogue for entries declared under functions: whose callee name is PascalCase -- the shape py.py:5942 types as instantiates. IMPORTANT NEGATIVE FIRST: go returns 162 hits and is NOT implicated by this mechanism. Go capitalises every EXPORTED identifier, so os.Open / net.Dial / os/exec.Command are PascalCase for reasons that have nothing to do with construction, and the PascalCase heuristic is Python-specific (it lives in py.py, not in go.py). Counting go here would have inflated the finding roughly nine-fold on a naming convention. Whether go has an ANALOGOUS path to instantiates is a separate unmeasured question, not this one. PYTHON, the language the heuristic lives in: 19 catalogued entries are candidates by the same mechanism, spanning FIVE boundary kinds rather than just subprocess -- subprocess: Popen (the one MEASURED). fs_write: tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile, tempfile.TemporaryFile, tempfile.TemporaryDirectory, tempfile.SpooledTemporaryFile, gzip.GzipFile, io.FileIO, logging.FileHandler, tarfile.TarFile, zipfile.ZipFile. fs_read: argparse.FileType, fileinput.FileInput, gzip.GzipFile, io.FileIO, tarfile.TarFile, zipfile.Path, zipfile.ZipFile. net_send: urllib.request.Request. logging: logging.StreamHandler. So the blind spot is not only the command-injection question: it also covers 'does untrusted data choose a path we write to' (fs_write) and the SSRF question (net_send urllib.request.Request). CANDIDATES, NOT FINDINGS. Only Popen has a live repro. The other 18 share the mechanism but each needs its own arm, because reaching the py.py branch depends on the import form -- the branch keys on receiver_name in module_imports, and a dotted receiver like urllib.request may or may not bind there. Do not quote 19 as a measured number; quote 1 measured, 18 candidates. Also unswept: elixir/objc/swift each returned 1 PascalCase hit and were not examined, and no non-Python analyzer was checked for an analogous construction heuristic."  # 53dc
